at  \\[35\\cdot40=1400.\\]Find some integer $n$ with $0\\leq n<1399$ such that $n$ is the multiplicative inverse to 160 modulo 1399.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"0e14dcac73cf2d5f2613b6327a479b2d836f845b6c37d4f9ab49942cce215068"},"seconds":61.827300786972046,"seed":245966643242582677,"stop":"eos","text":"To find an integer \\( n \\) with \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) such that \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of \\( 160 \\) modulo \\( 1399 \\), we need to find \\( n \\) such that:\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nThis problem can be solved using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm, which helps in finding the coefficients \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) such that:\n\\[ ax + by = \\gcd(a, b) \\]\n\nHere, \\( a = 160 \\) and \\( b = 1399 \\). We need to find \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) such that:\n\\[ 160x + 1399y = 1 \\]\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\\[\n1399 = 160 \\cdot 8 + 119\n\\]\n\\[\n160 = 119 \\cdot 1 + 41\n\\]\n\\[\n119 = 41 \\cdot 2 + 37\n\\]\n\\[\n41 = 37 \\cdot 1 + 4\n\\]\n\\[\n37 = 4 \\cdot 9 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n4 = 1 \\cdot 4 + 0\n\\]\n\nThe gcd is 1, confirming that 160 and 1399 are coprime, and an inverse exists. Now, we backtrack using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399.\n\nStarting from:\n\\[\n1 = 37 - 4 \\cdot 9\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( 4 \\) from \\( 4 = 41 - 37 \\):\n\\[\n1 = 37 - (41 - 37) \\cdot 9 = 37 - 41 \\cdot 9 + 37 \\cdot 9 = 37 \\cdot 10 - 41 \\cdot 9\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( 37 \\) from \\( 37 = 119 - 41 \\cdot 2 \\):\n\\[\n1 = (119 - 41 \\cdot 2) \\cdot 10 - 41 \\cdot 9 = 119 \\cdot 10 - 41 \\cdot 20 - 41 \\cdot 9 = 119 \\cdot 10 - 41 \\cdot 29\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( 41 \\) from \\( 41 = 160 - 119 \\):\n\\[\n1 = 119 \\cdot 10 - (160 - 119) \\cdot 29 = 119 \\cdot 10 - 160 \\cdot 29 + 119 \\cdot 29 = 119 \\cdot 39 - 160 \\cdot 29\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( 119 \\) from \\( 119 = 1399 - 160 \\cdot 8 \\):\n\\[\n1 = (1399 - 160 \\cdot 8) \\cdot 39 - 160 \\cdot 29 = 1399 \\cdot 39 - 160 \\cdot 312 - 160 \\cdot 29 = 1399 \\cdot 39 - 160 \\cdot 341\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n1 = 1399 \\cdot 39 - 160 \\cdot 341\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[\n-341 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399 is:\n\\[\nn = -341 \\pmod{1399}\n\\]\n\nTo find the positive equivalent, we add 1399 to -341:\n\\[\n-341 + 1399 = 1058\n\\]\n\nThus, the integer \\( n \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1058}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1058}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
